Date: Sun, 28 Feb 1993 01:43:23 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: TECH/ECON: Space colonization, a new approach

Some possibilities for space industrialization get less
attention than they deserve due to their negative
reputation.  One example is the military use of space,
especially for spying, which to this day dominates commercial 
applications in funding.  Another is the likely to be the largest
space industry, an industry which could make more money
from far less up-front investment than asteroid mining or solar
power satellites.

This industry -- recreational drugs -- also dominated the settlement
of our last great frontier, the Americas.  Most of the early 
English new world colonies were started to make tobacco or rum.

Space doesn't seem to hold any big advantages for drug
manufacture, although microgravity might prove useful for
some purposes, and the abundant solar energy will allow
space greenhouses to dwarf earthside farms in the long run.

But making drugs in space does bring one very important, immediate
advantage: direct delivery.  Just as a spy satellite knows no border, 
so a reentry vehicle knows no border guards.  Just as it has proved
difficult to defend against even small numbers of incoming nuclear 
warheads, so it may be prohibitive to strike down large numbers
of cheap, disposable reentry vehicles, sintered from lunar or
asteroid regolith, sometimes as a decoy and sometimes carrying its 
valuable cargo directly to the local distributor, anywhere on the planet.
Within minutes of its detection by NORAD, a reentry vehicle has arrived
at its exact location and its valuable (>$1,000/kg) payload taken
away by the local dealers.  In return, the dealers finance resupply
launches to the space industries via an encrypted digital black market,
perhaps fronting as a legal space pharmaceuticals manufacturer.

There are various ways to obtain the raw materials needed to grow
and process drugs in space.  In sci.space articles I have described
cometing mining, ice rockets, and the bootstrapping process by
which the cost of volatiles and organics in high earth orbit can
be reduced to a few cents per kilogram.  Such volatiles may also
be available at the lunar poles, Mar's moons, or certain earth-crossing
asteroids.  Alternatively, the crops can be grown in large bubbles
on Mars itself.  A Mars-fueled SSTO combined with an ice rocket
makes cargo shipment to earth very cheap, a few dollars per kilogram.

Once the volatiles and organics have been separated, they are
fed to a series of chemical microreactors on that topic) and 
converted in essential nutrients and construction materials for 
greenhouses.  Greenhouses are made in a very simple, automated 
fashion, for example by pumping air into liquid polymer spheres 
which are then solidified and filled with nutrients and trellises 
for the crop.  The crops include not only cannibis, coca, etc. but 
hemp fiber is also used to provide structural strength for further 
greenhouses, and genetically engineered enzymes are extracted and 
used in the chemical microreactors.  This self-replicating greenhouse 
system expands exponentially across the volatile ices of the inner solar
system, converting them into drugs and vehicles for delivering
them to Earth.

At final approach to Earth, the cargo vessel makes last-minute
adjustments and screams down to its destination, until the last
minute when high-g parachutes brake the cargo to a gentle landing.
At the same time other elements of the "meteor shower", decoys,
rain down at various nearby locations, diluting law enforcement 
resources in the area.  For further stealth, the deliveries might be 
timed to correspond with actual meteor showers.

The worldwide drug business comprises several hundred billion
dollars per year, and we might reasonably expect the ET biz
to take $100 billion per year of that business.  Compare to
the worldwide platinum-group element market ($3 billion) and
the worldwide satellite communications market ($4 billion) and
we see here a major potential for greatly expanding space activities.
Furthermore, most of the tech needed for self-sufficient space
colonies is developed in the self-replicating greenhouse system
for growing drug crops.  

D.D. Harriman may be a pusher!

